/* CHART: where each paycheck goes, one bar per place on ONE shared scale so the lengths compare.
   Money SPENT is drawn hollow and money LEFT OVER is the only solid fill. That is a shape
   difference, not a colour one, and it is deliberate: the site's cool grays sit too close to
   the blue and teal accents (normal-vision Delta E 8.5 to 13.6 against a floor of 15, run
   through the palette validator), so no flat neutral could separate "spent" from "kept" on
   colour alone. Hollow against solid reads for everyone. */
.ac-pc { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:18px; margin:6px 0 0; }
.ac-pc-row { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:6px; }
.ac-pc-name { font-size:var(--t-ui); color:var(--t2); display:flex; align-items:center; gap:8px; }
.ac-pc-track { position:relative; }
/* A GRID, not a flex row. 2026-09-22, David: "some of the scaling on the chart is messed up".
   As a flex row each segment took its share of 100% and THEN the 2px gaps and 8px of side
   padding were added on top, so every bar ran long: 6px over with three gaps, and about 22px
   over when a $9 "left over" sliver could not shrink below its own padding, which pushed
   California's bar past the edge of the card. In a grid the gaps and minimums come OUT of the
   bar's width before the fr columns are shared, so a bar can never be longer than its value. */
.ac-pc-bar { display:grid; gap:2px; height:30px; }
.ac-pc-seg { position:relative; min-width:0; display:flex; align-items:center; overflow:hidden;
